# Line endings are a repository decision, not a per-developer one.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true at the system level, so without
# this file every Windows contributor gets a CRLF working copy. That breaks two
# things in this repo specifically:
#
#   1. Shell scripts executed inside Linux containers or Git Bash — a CRLF
#      `#!/usr/bin/env bash` line fails with `$'\r': command not found`.
#   2. scripts/check_env.sh — it skips blank lines with [[ -z "$key" ]], but a
#      blank line in a CRLF file parses as "\r", not empty, so every blank line
#      in a .env.example is reported as a missing key.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` forces LF in the
# repository *and* in the working copy, regardless of anyone's core.autocrlf.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Run inside Linux containers — must always be LF, never auto-detected.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Submission artifacts: byte-for-byte, never transformed.
*.pptx binary
*.pdf binary
